 had on the plane  the wing area of the spit is noticeably  larger this affects a performance metric  called Wing loading which is simply the  total mass of the aircraft divided by  the wing area this affects the turning  radius of the aircraft a heavier  aircraft with smaller Wings will have a  larger Wing loading and as a result  we'll have a larger turning radius the  bf-109 wings were much smaller and thus  had a higher Wing loading and so the  bf-109 had a larger turn radius than the  Spitfire  while the space afforded by the  spitfire's wings allowed it to fit eight  Browning machine guns inside the wing or  as later variants featured two larger 20  millimeter cannons and four of the  smaller 7.7 millimeter brownies all  mounted inside the wing and outside the  propeller Arc which reduced the  engineering effort needed to integrate  them a luxury the bf-109 did not have  the engineers at messerschmith were  forced to use more complicated designs  to integrate ornaments two machine guns  mounted above the engine could fire  through the propeller blades with an  interrupter gear timing the triggering  to ensure they did not strike the blades  a cannon could be fitted behind the  engine which would fire through a hollow  tube that ran through the inverted V12  engine and through the nose of the  propeller Hub later variants attempted  to correct the disparity in Firepower by  mounting guns inside the wings but there  wasn't enough room for ammunition so a  belt feeder was run through a shoot from  the fuselage to the guns this wasn't a  reliable mechanism some variants did  include gun pods mounted below the wing  which negatively affected the plane's  handling the spitfire's primary role was  an Interceptor to do this it needed a  powerful engine and light airframe the  most important factor for climb rate is  power to rate ratio creating a powerful  engine that could fit into a small  airframe required some of the best mines  in Britain the Merlin engine of the  Spitfire went through many iterations  over the war with the constant goal to  squeeze more and more power out of the  27 liter displacement engine  displacement meaning the volume of the  cylinder swept by each piston the  displacement of an engine has a huge  effect on the total power output power  is derived from fuel burning to create  heat which creates pressure on the  cylinders to do work to increase power  we need to increase the amount of heat  energy released but we can't just add  more fuel without adding more air to  burn it there is an Optimum air fuel  ratio about 12 to 1 12 Parts air to one  part fuel this gets even more  complicated for aircraft that fly  through various altitudes because the  air pressure decreases as we climb air  pressure drops by half at 5 500 meters  which would half the oxygen available to  burn the fuel to combat this the Merlin  featured a supercharger to increase air  pressure air began its journey into the  Merlin engine through the air intake  located underneath the fuselage from  here it first passes the carburetor  where fuel is mixed into the Airstream  the Spitfire utilized a float carburetor  float carburetor is used as the name  would suggest a float the float Works  similarly to the float in your toilet  cistern detecting the fluid level and  controlling a valve to keep it at the  optimum level in the carburetor tank  this became an issue for the Spitfire  when German Pilots learned the engine  would cut out in a negative G maneuver a  negative G maneuver would force the  float down and open the valve flooding  the engine with too much fuel the bf-109  engine utilized direct fuel injection a  more complicated system that required 12  plungers one for each cylinder cams to  time the fuel injection and geared power  from the engine a more complicated  solution that allowed German Pilots to  perform negative Jeep Dives to evade  pursues while Spitfire Pilots needed  extra time to perform a roll before  diving which ensured the dive was made  in positive giving German Pilots  precious time to escape after passing  the carburetor the air entered the  supercharger compressor where the air  was compressed before entering the  Piston increasing the power output of  the engine this system helped the  Spitfire squeeze out extra horsepower  from their 27 liter engine rivaling the  power of the much larger 34 liter db601  engine fitted into the bf-109 during the  Battle of Britain however early Merlin  engines featured a single speed single  stage supercharger the speed of the  supercharger being determined by the  gearing ratio coming from the crankshaft  this forced the engineers to design the  supercharger to work at the optimum  speed and compression ratio for a  particular altitude we can see this when  looking at Power curves that plot the  engine power versus altitude  early Merlin 3 reached Peak power around  10 000 feet before quickly dropping off  you may notice that power output is  actually lower at sea level despite the  ambient air pressure being higher here  that's also a result of the supercharger  speed being fixed in order to prevent  the engine from being over pressured  there was a throttle on the air intake  that gradually opened as the plane  gained altitude while the Spitfire had  excellent power at its Optimum altitude  its bf-109 foes during the Battle of  Britain had Superior power at lower  altitudes because they featured an  ingenious device that allowed their  superchargers to vary their speed their  supercharger was placed at a 90 degree  angle to the engine and power was  transferred to it with a fluid coupling  which uses a fluid to transfer power  from the engine to the supercharger the  input shaft causes a fluid inside the  coupling to rotate around the casing  with a pump which in turn causes is a  turbine attached to the output shaft to  turn  this method of power transmission  allowed the supercharger to operate at  various speeds because the amount of oil  inside the coupling was controlled by a  separate pump which gradually increased  the amount of oil inside the coupling as  the plane gained altitude this prevented  the supercharger from over pressuring  the engine and gave the bf-109 engine  Superior power at lower altitudes  this gave the bf-109e variant which was  most widely used during the Battle of  Britain an edge over the Spitfire while  the Spitfire could outturn the 109 the  109 had a better power to rate ratio  which allowed the 109 to sustain a  climbing turn to outmaneuver a Spitfire  on its tail British Engineers were well  aware of this issue prior to the Battle  of Britain and development began on a  two-stage two-speed supercharger for the  Merlin engine in mid-1940. this allowed  the supercharger to operate at two  different speeds via a gear change but  also introduced a second impeller which  increased the compression for even  better high altitude performance this  increased compression caused an increase  in temperature of the air that would  negatively affect engine efficiency and  so an intercooler had to be introduced  to cool The Supercharged air before  entering during the piston this  intercooler removed 33 percent of the  heat added by the supercharger removing  more heat would have been beneficial but  there is a balancing act here to remove  more heat the plane needed a larger  radiator to dump that heat to atmosphere  and a larger radiator would result in  more drag which negated the power  increase  the radiator design in fact was one of  the chief ways the Spitfire managed to  squeeze so much power out of its smaller  Merlin engine German Engineers had noted  how much smaller the spitfire's  radiators were a knew exactly why in one  meeting between the chiefs of Germany's  Aviation development messerschmith noted  that the spitfire's radiators were half  the size of their own while the head of  German engine development explained to  an indignant government official that to  reduce the radiator size would require  higher coolant pressures which their  engine couldn't tolerate without leaking  the British had developed high pressure  high temperature coolant systems for  their high-speed Racing Engines to  compete in races like the Schneider  trophy the Germans were prohibited from  participating in these Races by the  Treaty of Versailles and so were left  behind in the development of this vital  technology apology increasing the  pressure of the coolant allowed the  engineers to raise the operating  temperature of the coolant without it  boiling an increased temperature  difference between the radiator and the  outside air increased the rate heat  could be radiated away from the plane  and this allowed the spitfire's radiator  to be much smaller this did require the  radiators to be stronger and thus  heavier but the reduction in drag more  than made up for the increased weight  the bf-109 and spitfire were formidable  foes for each other and the difference  between life and death more often than  not came down to the skill of the pilots  and the Strategic advantages the British  had in fighting over their home soil the  Battle of Britain was one on small  margins and the engineers of the  Spitfire did everything they could to  give the Spitfire an edge in battle but  it and the much more numerous Hawker  hurricane would not have stood a chance  without the systems and procedures on  the ground that allowed them to get into  the Air day after day radar data  processing mapping chain of commands  pilot training and even fuel octane  ratings all played a role in winning the  Battle of Britain and I explain it all  and our nebula original series The  Battle of Britain many of the animated  sets including the custom-made  operations room at Bentley Priory that  you saw in this video were created for  that series and that's the level of  quality you can expect from all our  nebula original series there are  currently four episodes available for  you to watch right now with even more on  the way along with their seven part  Logistics of D-Day series or you could  watch my favorite recent original by  Wendover Productions covering the  history of Wake Island a tiny remote  island in the middle of the Pacific with  a rich history in aviation and World War  II if you want to support this Channel  watch our videos ad free including  sponsor messages like this and get to  watch incredible exclusive documentaries  from independent creators join nebula  Now by signing up for the Curiosity  stream and nebula bundle deal for just  14.79 a year  1479 for an entire year membership  giving you access to both nebula and  curiosity streams massive catalog of  educational and entertaining videos and  documentaries

